---INSTRUCTION---
Write an Elixir function that calculates the maximum profit achievable by buying and selling a stock on different days, given a list of prices. You are allowed to make as many transactions as you like (buy one and sell one share multiple times). The function should return the total profit.

The function `max_profit/1` takes a list of non-negative integers representing stock prices on consecutive days and returns an integer representing the maximum profit.

If the list is empty or has only one day, no transaction can be made, so the profit is 0.

Examples:
- For prices [7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4], the maximum profit is 7: buy at 1, sell at 5 (profit 4), then buy at 3, sell at 6 (profit 3), total 7.
- For prices [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], the maximum profit is 4: buy at 1, sell at 5.
- For prices [7, 6, 4, 3, 1], the maximum profit is 0: no profitable transactions.
---TEST---
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "empty list" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([]) == 0
  end

  test "single element" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([10]) == 0
  end

  test "two elements, profit possible" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([1, 5]) == 4
  end

  test "two elements, no profit" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([5, 1]) == 0
  end

  test "example 1" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4]) == 7
  end

  test "example 2" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 4
  end

  test "example 3" do
    assert Solution.max_profit([7, 6, 4, 3, 1]) == 0
  end
end
---REFERENCE---
defmodule Solution do
  def max_profit([]), do: 0
  def max_profit([_]), do: 0

  def max_profit(prices) do
    prices
    |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
    |> Enum.reduce(0, fn [a, b], acc ->
      if b > a do
        acc + (b - a)
      else
        acc
      end
    end)
  end
end
